The problem involves finding the ratio of the shaded area to the area of a circle with radius \( CD \) as the diameter. The key steps are to find the radius of the semicircles and the length of \( CD \). The radius of the semicircles can be found from the collinear points \( O_1 \), \( O_2 \), and \( O_3 \) which are their centers, and the distances between them. To find \( CD \), use the Pythagorean theorem on triangle \( \triangle OCD \): Since \( O_1 \) and \( O_2 \) are opposite, they have the same distance. Now, by finding the crucial distance between \( O_1 \) and \( O_2 \), you can deduce the length of \( CD \).

\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Given}: \quad O_1 C+O_1 D+O_2 D+O_2 C+O_3 C+O_3 D+O_1 D+O_1 C=AC+BC-OO_1+AB-O_3 O_1=2 \times CD \\
\Rightarrow 3(AC+BC)+OO_1=2 \times CD.
\end{aligned}
\]

\textit{Determine the distances:}
\[
\begin{aligned}
2(AC)+OO_1+2(CB)= 3(AC+CB)+OO_1+OO_1= 2 \times CD, \\
\Rightarrow OD+OO_1=CD.
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{think} <answer> 1:4 </answer>

